"""THE HISTORY OF LEGO — PART ELEVEN: THE GEOMETRY OF A BRICK

Everything about the brick is a number, and the numbers were fixed long ago. The distance from the centre of one stud to the centre of the next is eight millimetres. A stud is 4.8 millimetres across and 1.7 millimetres high. A standard brick is 9.6 millimetres tall without its studs, and a plate, the thin element, is exactly one third of that, 3.2 millimetres, so that three plates stacked equal one brick. A two-by-four brick is 15.8 millimetres wide rather than sixteen, because each brick is made 0.1 millimetre narrower than the grid on every side, leaving a gap of a fifth of a millimetre between neighbours. Without that gap a long wall would bind and bow; with it, a wall of a hundred bricks stays straight.

The grip that holds a model together is called clutch power inside the company. It comes from the interference between the studs of one brick and the walls and tubes of the brick above: the stud is fractionally larger than the space it enters, the plastic flexes by a few hundredths of a millimetre, and friction does the rest. Too much and a child cannot pull the bricks apart; too little and a model collapses when lifted. The tolerance the moulds must hold is about two thousandths of a millimetre, roughly the thickness of a soap bubble. A brick that drifts out of that tolerance goes back into the granulator to be melted again.

The three tubes in a two-by-four brick are not decoration. Each one sits between four studs of the brick beneath and touches all four, so that the brick is gripped at eight points along its walls and twelve at its tubes. A two-by-two brick has one tube; a one-by-four has three small pins instead, because a single row of studs has no space between studs for a tube. The mathematics of where the tubes go was worked out in Billund in 1957, tested with prototypes made in wood and brass, and written into the patent of January 1958.

The material has changed once. Cellulose acetate, used until 1963, was easy to colour and mould but absorbed moisture from the air and slowly warped, which is why the oldest bricks in collections lean slightly. ABS, the plastic adopted in the early 1960s, is a mixture of three components: acrylonitrile for hardness and chemical resistance, butadiene for toughness so that the brick does not shatter, and styrene for gloss and ease of moulding. It arrives at the factory as small beads, is heated to around 230 degrees Celsius, and is injected into the mould at pressures measured in tonnes. The cycle takes about ten seconds, and the part comes out at nearly its final temperature, needing only to cool.

The colour is in the plastic, not on it. Pigment is mixed with the granules before moulding, so that a scratched brick is the same colour all the way through. This is also why the palette is limited: every colour is a separate stock of material, and every element in every colour is a separate item to store and pick. In the early years there were five colours. By the late 1990s there were more than one hundred, which was one of the causes of the crisis. Today there are around sixty, and the argument over whether a new one is necessary still takes place in a committee.

A brick from 1958 will fit a brick from this year because the numbers have not moved. This is the quiet reason the system survived every fashion: the company could change what the sets depicted, from castles to spaceships to films, without changing the grid beneath them.""",

"""THE HISTORY OF LEGO — PART TWELVE: HOW A BRICK IS MADE

The main factory still stands in Billund, a few hundred metres from the site of the original workshop, and it is the largest single factory in the group, though not the only one. Bricks are moulded in Billund in Denmark, in Nyíregyháza in eastern Hungary, in Kladno near Prague, in Monterrey in Mexico, in Jiaxing near Shanghai, and, from the middle of the 2020s, in a new plant in Vietnam and another under construction in the state of Virginia. Each factory serves mostly its own region, so that a box sold in Germany was probably moulded in Hungary and a box sold in Texas in Mexico.

Inside a moulding hall there are hundreds of injection machines in long rows, and almost no people. Each machine holds a steel mould, itself weighing anything from a few hundred kilograms to several tonnes, machined to the tolerance of a few thousandths of a millimetre and costing as much as a family house. A mould might produce a single element or a small family of them, and a machine can run day and night for weeks on one mould before it is changed. The granules arrive by pipe from silos on the roof, and the finished parts drop into bins that a robot carries away when a sensor says the bin is full.

The company likes to say that about eighteen elements in every million fail inspection, and the figure is plausible: the process has been refined for seventy years, and the same moulds are checked and repaired continuously. Failed parts and the small runners of plastic left over from moulding are ground and remelted.

The second half of the factory is about counting. A set with a thousand pieces is not assembled by hand; it is assembled by machines that drop the correct number of each element into small bags, weighing every bag to check that nothing is missing or extra. The weighing is precise enough to notice a single missing one-by-one plate. Sets are packed with the bags numbered so that a builder opens them in order, an innovation that arrived in the 2000s and reduced the hours spent searching for pieces in a pile.

Design of an element begins as a sketch, then a computer model, then a prototype made on a 3D printer, and finally a test mould. Before a new element is approved, someone has to argue that no existing element could do the job, because every new element is a new mould in every factory that makes it, and every colour of that element is another stock to keep. This is the discipline that the crisis of 2003 imposed and that the company has kept.

There is one more test that every element passes, and it has nothing to do with clutch power. The company tests whether a piece can be swallowed and lodge in a small throat, whether it can splinter, whether the paint on a minifigure's face will come off in a mouth, and what happens when a brick is bitten, stepped on or dropped. The standard is written into international toy regulations, but the company's own limits are tighter, for the simple reason that its product is sold to be chewed by toddlers in a way that few products are.

The factories run largely on renewable electricity by the company's own accounting, and the newest ones were designed to be carbon neutral in operation. The plastic remains the unsolved part of that story, which is the subject of a later chapter.""",

"""THE HISTORY OF LEGO — PART THIRTEEN: THE DESIGNERS

A set does not begin with a brick. It begins with a question asked of children, and the company employs people whose job is to sit on the floor of a playroom in Billund, or in a rented house in Germany or the United States, and watch how a seven-year-old plays with a prototype and where the play stops. The observations go back to the designers, of whom there are several hundred, drawn from dozens of countries, many of them recruited from adult fan communities where their models had been noticed.

The colour palette they work with was reformed in 2004 as part of the turnaround, and the change is still argued about by collectors. Old grey and old dark grey were replaced by bluish greys, and old brown by a redder brown, so that models would look cleaner under shop lighting and photograph better. Builders with large collections of the old greys found that new sets no longer matched their bricks, and twenty years later the two families of grey are still sorted separately in every serious collection.

The design process for a set of the City range takes about a year. A theme is planned two or three years ahead, with characters, story and a range of price points: a small set for pocket money, a middle one for a birthday, a large one for the holidays. Each set is built and rebuilt, given to children, changed, costed, tested for stability, and only then does someone design the box and write the instructions.

Instructions are a discipline of their own. Since the 1960s the company has avoided words in the building steps, so that the same booklet serves every language. Each step adds a small number of pieces, shown in a call-out at the top, and the model is drawn from an angle at which every new piece is visible. Mistakes in instructions are rare and are remembered by fans for years. The digital versions on the company's application add the ability to rotate the model, and were introduced in part because the paper booklets of the largest sets had grown to several hundred pages.

The model shop in Billund is a separate world. Its builders make the giant models for the parks, for shops and for events: a life-size elephant, a Star Wars fighter with more than five million bricks, a working car that could carry a person. The large models are designed on computers that convert a shape into a brick-by-brick plan, then built by hand and glued, which the company does nowhere else. The biggest models take months and travel by lorry in sections.

Element design is separate from set design. A new element, such as a curved slope or a minifigure accessory, is proposed when several set designers ask for the same thing, and a small team spends months making sure it fits every existing element it could touch. The rule that a new piece must connect properly to the whole system, in every orientation a child might try, is the reason that the catalogue contains far fewer strange parts than a company of this size might be expected to produce.

The designers are also the people who answer the question that every fan asks: why is a particular set the price it is. The answer is usually the number of moulds, the number of colours and the size of the box rather than the number of bricks, and the plain answer has never satisfied anyone.""",

"""THE HISTORY OF LEGO — PART FOURTEEN: TOWN, THEN CITY

Of all the themes, the one that has never gone away is the one that depicts nothing exotic at all. Town, launched in 1978 alongside Castle and Space, was a place where fire engines went to fires, ambulances went to hospitals, and a petrol station sold petrol. It was the first world the minifigure lived in, and in every decade since, the best-selling sets of the year have usually been a police station or a fire station.

The early Town of the late 1970s and 1980s was built on road plates, printed baseplates with roads that fitted together into a grid, and its buildings were small: a garage, a post office, a café. The vehicles were the point, and the company issued them by the dozen, from a milk float to a tow truck. The airport of 1985 was a landmark set, with a passenger plane whose fuselage was built from bricks rather than a single moulded shell, which the company regarded as a point of honour.

In the 1990s Town grew sub-themes: Paradisa with pink and pastel colours aimed at girls, Divers and Res-Q, a coastguard, and Town Jr., which used larger pre-moulded parts so that younger children could finish a model quickly. Town Jr. was popular with parents and unpopular with everyone who remembered building an aeroplane from a hundred small pieces, and in retrospect it was an early symptom of the drift that ended in the crisis.

In 2005, during the turnaround, the theme was rebuilt and renamed City. The change was more than a name. The sets went back to proper building, the vehicles gained realistic proportions, and the range was planned as a coherent town with fire, police, construction, harbour, airport, trains and later a space port, a jungle expedition, an Arctic base and a mine. City became the largest theme in the company's catalogue, larger than Star Wars in most years, and the annual police station is still the safest bet in the toy industry.

City also became a test bed for what a minifigure could be. In the 2010s City sets included figures in wheelchairs, figures with beards and glasses, and a wider range of jobs; in the 2020s the company began to include figures with limb differences and hearing aids, and it did so quietly, in sets where the figure was simply a person doing a job.

The theme's advertising is a story of its own. From the 1980s the television commercials for Town and later City were built in the style of a child playing, with the camera at table level and a voice narrating a rescue, and the format changed so little over thirty years that adults who grew up with it recognised the same rhythm in the adverts their children watched. The company has said that this was deliberate: City is the theme where a parent should recognise the toy they had.

Trains belong here as well. LEGO trains arrived in 1966 with a small motor, moved to a 12-volt system with proper track in 1980, to 9-volt in 1991, to remote control in 2006 and to battery power with Bluetooth in the late 2010s. Each change made old track incompatible with new trains and produced a wave of protests from the collectors who had built basements around the old standard. The company's response, each time, was to move on, which is the one area in which it has broken its own rule about everything fitting everything.""",

"""THE HISTORY OF LEGO — PART FIFTEEN: CASTLES

The first castle was yellow. In 1978 the company released a castle set with yellow walls, because grey bricks were not yet made in quantity, and with knights whose helmets and visors were the first specialised minifigure parts. It had four towers, a drawbridge on a chain, and a base that hinged open so that a child could play inside. Older fans still call it the Yellow Castle, and it is one of the sets that appears in every history of the company.

Grey arrived in 1984 with the Lion Knights and the Black Falcons, two factions distinguished by their shields, and a castle that was suddenly the colour of stone. The knights got horses, the first moulded animals in the system, and the horses got barding. In 1987 the Forestmen appeared: outlaws in green who lived in tree houses and hid in caves, obviously borrowed from Robin Hood without saying so. The theme was now a small world with sides, and children took sides.

Through the 1990s the factions multiplied. The Black Knights had a castle with a dragon on a rock; the Wolfpack were a band of renegades with wolves on their shields; the Dragon Masters of 1993 introduced wizards, magic and a large red dragon, the first fantasy elements in a theme that had been medieval history until then. The Royal Knights followed with a king, the Fright Knights with witches and bats, and the Ninja sub-theme of 1998 moved the whole thing to Japan with samurai and shoguns.

In 2000 the theme paused for the first time since 1978, a casualty of the years when the company chased other things, and the Knights' Kingdom that returned in 2004 was an action-figure line with large jointed figures, another symptom of the crisis. The rebuilt Castle of 2007 went back to bricks and stone-grey walls, with a Crown King against a Skeleton army, and in 2010 Kingdoms returned to knights against knights, lions against dragons, as the theme had been at its best.

The largest castle set of the classic era was the Royal Knight's Castle of 1995; the largest ever was released in 2022 for the company's ninetieth birthday, a Lion Knights' Castle of more than four thousand pieces designed as an explicit tribute to 1984, with the same shields, the same forestmen hiding outside the walls, and a section that hinged open just as the yellow one had done. Its designers were children when the original came out, and they said so in interviews.

Castle taught the company something that Town could not: a theme could be a story with villains. The sub-themes were the first attempts to give sets a plot, and the plot sold. Everything the company later did with Bionicle and Ninjago, the invented mythologies with their own films and series, descends from the moment in 1984 when a child had to decide whether to be a Lion Knight or a Black Falcon.

There is a footnote for collectors. The horses of 1984 were made from a single moulded piece that could not move; a horse with a movable head arrived only in 2012. Thirty years of knights rode statues, and nobody complained, because the horse was not the point.""",

"""THE HISTORY OF LEGO — PART SIXTEEN: SPACE

Classic Space, the theme launched in 1978, had a look that has never been surpassed for economy: grey and blue bricks, transparent yellow windscreens, a logo of a planet with a ring and a rocket arc, and astronauts in red, white, yellow, blue and, later, black suits, all with the same smiling face and a gold visor that lifted. The sets were spaceships and moon bases on grey crater baseplates, with an optimism about space travel that belonged to the decade after the Moon landings. There was no enemy. Everyone was an explorer.

That changed in 1987 when Blacktron appeared in black and yellow with a sinister logo, and Futuron in white and blue as the new heroes, and in 1989 when the Space Police arrived with prison cells built into their ships to lock Blacktron figures inside. The theme had learned from Castle that sides sold. Through the 1990s the sub-themes came quickly: M-Tron in red and black with magnets, Blacktron II with a redesign, Ice Planet 2002 with orange skis and transparent neon-orange windows, Spyrius with spies and robots, Unitron, Exploriens with holographic prints, Roboforce, Insectoids with glowing green parts, and Life on Mars in 2001 with actual Martians.

The theme then disappeared for a while, replaced by licensed space in the form of Star Wars, and when it came back in 2007 as Mars Mission and later as Space Police III and Alien Conquest, the original blue and grey palette was gone. Galaxy Squad in 2013 was the last of the line for many years, and the company folded space exploration into City, where astronauts have lived since with shuttles, rovers and a lunar base, in colours borrowed from real space agencies rather than from 1978.

Classic Space itself never quite died, because its fans would not let it. When The LEGO Movie was made in 2014, one of its main characters was Benny, a 1980s Classic Space astronaut in a blue suit with a cracked helmet, the crack being an accurate detail: the early helmets had a thin chin strap that broke on almost every figure. Benny's obsession with building spaceships was the theme's own obsession, and it introduced the look to children born thirty years after the sets had gone.

Adult fans took the theme further. The Classic Space logo was reissued on new sets, a large Galaxy Explorer in the original colours was released in 2022 for the ninetieth anniversary, and the company's own designers have said in public that they keep a shelf of 1979 sets in the office as a standard for what a spaceship should look like.

The lesson of Space is the opposite of the lesson of Castle. Castle showed that a story sold a theme. Space showed that a look could outlive every story attached to it: the sub-themes with their villains and holograms are forgotten, and the plain grey ship with a yellow window is the one that a designer keeps on the shelf.""",

"""THE HISTORY OF LEGO — PART SEVENTEEN: PIRATES, ADVENTURERS AND THE REST

Pirates arrived in 1989 and broke two rules at once. The minifigures had faces that were not the standard smile, with beards, eye patches and expressions, and the figures had hooks and peg legs, the first minifigure body variations. The ships were the largest models the company had made, with cloth sails, rigging, a hull built from large curved sections that were a new kind of element, and cannons that fired small plastic balls with a spring, until safety regulations later made them fire nothing. The Black Seas Barracuda of 1989 became one of the most valued sets among collectors and was reissued in 2002 to celebrate its own legend.

The pirates fought the Imperial Soldiers, redcoats with a fort and a governor, and later the Imperial Armada in Spanish style, and in 1994 they met the Islanders, natives of a tropical island with a king, masks and a giant idol. The theme ran to 1997, returned for two years in 2009, and has been revived for a single large set every so often since, most recently as a pirate ship designed by a fan through the Ideas programme.

Western in 1996 gave the minifigure a cowboy hat and a horse to match, with a fort, a town and a sheriff, and lasted only two years. Adventurers in 1998 followed a hero called Johnny Thunder from Egypt to the Amazon to Dino Island and the Orient, essentially the plots of adventure films without the licence, and it introduced dinosaurs, mummies and a villain with a monocle. It was one of the last original story themes before the crisis, and one of the best.

Underwater themes were a company habit. Aquazone in 1995 had two factions, Aquanauts and Aquasharks, in submarines with transparent domes; Hydronauts and Stingrays followed; Atlantis in 2010 sent divers after a lost city with shark warriors; and Aqua Raiders in 2007 fought giant sea creatures. None of them lasted more than three years, and the company appears to have concluded that children like the sea less than it hoped.

Rock Raiders in 1999 put miners on an alien planet with drilling vehicles and rock monsters, and had a video game that many players remember more fondly than the sets. Alpha Team in 2001 was a spy story with a villain called Ogel, whose name is the company's name reversed. Both were the products of the years when the company was trying anything.

There were also the themes for smaller hands: Fabuland from 1979 to 1989, with animal characters larger than minifigures, houses and a television series in some countries; and Belville and Scala for girls in the 1990s, with taller dolls. Fabuland is remembered with affection by people now in their forties, and the animal figures fetch prices that surprise their owners.

The point of listing them is not nostalgia. Every one of these themes was a bet on what children wanted to pretend to be, and most of the bets lasted two or three years, which is the normal life of a theme. The unusual thing about the company is not that it had hits; it is that it kept the bricks the same underneath all of them, so that the pirate ship, the submarine and the spaceship could be broken up and rebuilt into something that none of the designers had planned.""",

"""THE HISTORY OF LEGO — PART EIGHTEEN: TECHNIC

The line that became Technic began in 1977 under the name Expert Builder, with four sets: a tractor, a forklift, a helicopter and a car chassis with a working engine whose pistons moved when the wheels turned. The pieces were beams with holes at the same eight-millimetre pitch as the studs, axles with a cross-shaped section, gears in several sizes, and connector pegs. A child who had outgrown houses could now build a gearbox.

The name changed to Technic in 1982, and in 1984 pneumatics arrived: a pump, cylinders and valves so that a crane or an excavator could lift by air pressure, a system that was refined twice more over the following decades. The first Technic figures appeared in 1986, larger than minifigures and with jointed limbs, so that a driver could sit in a car with proper proportions; they lasted until 2001 and are now collected in their own right.

The Supercar of 1994, set number 8880, is regarded by many as the high point of the studded era: a car with four-wheel steering, a four-speed gearbox, independent suspension on every wheel, a V8 engine with moving pistons and pop-up headlights, from around 1,300 pieces. It was the kind of set that was built once, kept on a shelf for years, and rebuilt by its owner as an adult.

In the late 1990s the line changed its bones. Studless beams, smooth on all sides with holes for pegs, gradually replaced the studded beams, and by the mid-2000s a Technic set was built almost entirely from them. Old builders complained that the new sets did not look like LEGO. New builders found that the models were stronger, lighter and closer in shape to the machines they copied. The argument continued for a decade and was settled by the sales figures.

Motors followed the same path as trains. Power Functions in 2007 brought a standard battery box, motors, lights and an infrared remote that could be built into any model; Control+ in 2019 replaced it with Bluetooth motors controlled from a phone application, and the change of connector made ten years of motors obsolete overnight, to the usual protests.

The licensed supercars of the 2010s and 2020s took the line into a new market. A Porsche 911 GT3 RS in 2016, a Bugatti Chiron in 2018, a Lamborghini Sián, a Ferrari Daytona and a McLaren P1 were models of around 3,500 to 4,000 pieces, sold at prices that only adults pay, with working gearboxes and, in some cases, packaging that opened like a car showroom. The Liebherr R 9800 excavator of 2019, with four motors and two Bluetooth hubs, was the most complex machine the company had ever sold, and the lorry crane that followed had a boom that extended more than a metre.

Technic also feeds the rest of the company. Its beams and pins are the skeleton inside the largest Star Wars ships and the internal frame of the Titanic, and the gears in a City crane come from the same moulds. It was designed in 1977 for teenagers who liked machines; it became the engineering department of the whole system.""",

"""THE HISTORY OF LEGO — PART NINETEEN: DUPLO AND THE YOUNGEST BUILDERS

The idea of a bigger brick for smaller hands was tested in Billund from the mid-1960s under the name Jumbo, and released in 1969 as Duplo. Every dimension is twice the classic brick, which gives eight times the volume, and the underside is shaped so that a classic brick's studs fit into it. A one-year-old and a nine-year-old can build on the same table, and the family's Duplo does not have to be given away when the child moves on; it becomes the base of the bigger structures.

The first Duplo sets were plain bricks in bright colours. In 1977 the first Duplo figures arrived, round and simple, and in 1983 the theme found its shape with houses, a zoo, a farm and a train with a push-along engine, which by the 1990s had a battery motor. A Duplo train set has been in the catalogue almost continuously since, and it is, by the company's own account, the toy that most often prompts a parent to say that their child's first word was the name of a colour.

The line for children below eighteen months was harder to get right. Primo, launched in 1995, used even larger elements with a different coupling, was renamed Baby, and was withdrawn; Quatro, in 2004, was four times the classic scale and lasted two years. The lesson was that a toddler does not need a third system, and that Duplo itself, with its soft edges and elements too large to swallow, already served the youngest.

Duplo has always been the theme where the company's research into learning shows most directly. Sets are designed around a developmental stage rather than a story: stacking, then sorting by colour, then building something that stands, then pretending. The Duplo figures were redesigned in 2004 to be more human and again in the 2010s to be more expressive, because studies in Billund found that toddlers talked to figures with faces they could read. Licensed characters entered Duplo carefully, first with Disney and later with Peppa Pig and superheroes, and always in the same simplified shapes.

The theme has a parallel life in schools. LEGO Education, the successor to Dacta, sells Duplo sets to kindergartens with cards for teachers, from a set of tubes and marbles that teaches cause and effect, to sets of faces that teach children to name emotions. In the 2010s the LEGO Foundation, which owns a quarter of the company, funded research on learning through play in early childhood across many countries, and much of it was done with Duplo.

There is a design detail that summarises the theme. The Duplo brick is hollow like the classic brick, but the tubes inside it are a single wide cylinder rather than the classic pattern, because a small child's fingers must be able to separate two bricks without help, and the clutch power was deliberately made weaker. A toy for a toddler was designed around the strength of a toddler's hand, which sounds obvious and took years to measure.

The Duplo name was briefly retired in 2002 when the company renamed the line Explore, as part of a plan to sort products by age instead of by brand. Parents did not understand the change, and the name came back in 2004. It was one of the first reversals of the turnaround.""",

"""THE HISTORY OF LEGO — PART TWENTY: BRICKS THAT THINK

The idea that a brick could contain a computer came from the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, where Seymour Papert and his colleagues had spent the 1980s putting children in front of the Logo programming language and arguing that they learned mathematics by building things. The company funded the work from 1985, and by the late 1980s a prototype called the programmable brick existed in the Media Lab: a yellow box with ports for motors and sensors, connected to a computer by cable.

The commercial version, LEGO Mindstorms, appeared in 1998 with a brick called the RCX. It had a small processor, three motor outputs, three sensor inputs, an infrared link to a personal computer and a graphical programming language in which a child dragged blocks together. The first robot most people built was a wheeled vehicle that turned when its bumper touched a wall. The kit cost around two hundred dollars and sold about eighty thousand units in its first year, far more than planned, and a large share went to adults who had no children.

Within weeks of release, enthusiasts had disassembled the RCX firmware and written their own operating systems for it in C and other languages, and posted them on the internet. The company's first instinct was legal. Its second, after some months, was to accept what had happened and later to write into the licence that owners could modify and share the software. Mindstorms became a case study in business schools for what a company gains by not suing its customers.

A second generation, the NXT, arrived in 2006 with a faster processor, a proper screen, Bluetooth, and sensors for sound, light and ultrasound distance, designed with a panel of adult fans who signed non-disclosure agreements and worked with the company for a year. The EV3 of 2013 ran a version of Linux and could be programmed from a tablet. The Robot Inventor of 2020 used the same hub as the school product Spike Prime and the programming language Scratch. In 2022 the company announced that Mindstorms would end at the close of that year, after twenty-four years, saying that its robotics work would continue through the education range.

The education side had been growing alongside. In 1998 the company and the inventor Dean Kamen, founder of the FIRST organisation, started the FIRST LEGO League, a competition in which teams of children build and program a robot to complete tasks on a table in two and a half minutes, and present a research project on a theme such as water or transport. It grew from a few hundred teams in the United States to tens of thousands of teams in more than a hundred countries, and it is the reason a large number of engineers under forty first wrote a program.

WeDo, for younger children, connected a small motor and a tilt sensor to a computer and let a nine-year-old make a crocodile that snapped when a hand came near. Spike Prime, launched in 2019, became the standard classroom robot, with a hub that had a five-by-five light matrix and a gyroscope, and is the product that inherited the Mindstorms work.

The programmable brick was in every sense the most ambitious product the company ever made: a toy that required a computer, a programming language and a manual of two hundred pages. It sold because it did what the plain brick did, only in another dimension. It gave the child parts and a suggestion and left the rest to them.""",
